    "title": "How to lace trail shoes for a wide forefoot",
    "metaDescription": "A lacing technique for wide feet.",
    "intro": "Skip the second eyelet from the toe and run the lace straight up the side instead of crossing it. That single change gives a wide forefoot room without loosening the heel.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The window lacing method",
        "body": "Cross the laces up to the point where the shoe pinches, then run them parallel through one pair of eyelets before crossing again above. The parallel section is the window; it stops pressure building over the widest part of the foot."
      },
      {
        "heading": "When lacing is not enough",
        "body": "If the upper still cuts in with the window open, the last is too narrow and no lacing pattern will fix it. The Ridge Wide and the Fell Wide are built on wider lasts; the standard Ridge is not."
      }
